WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.152
A photographed sketch of a room.

00:00:02.152 --> 00:00:03.003
No source file.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:06.406
Somebody has to redraw it in AutoCAD, one line at a time.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.052
Hi, I'm Burl!

00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:13.419
This open skill promises to do it for you, and to say

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:14.542
which numbers it guessed.

00:00:14.542 --> 00:00:15.664
I read every file.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.813
First, a quick check of the image: size, blur and contrast.

00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:23.498
Then the key step, and no script does it.

00:00:23.498 --> 00:00:26.545
The model looks at the picture and types the drawing as JSON.

00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:28.813
A validator checks the JSON's shape.

00:00:29.545 --> 00:00:34.478
Python drives AutoCAD over COM, one call per entity, and saves a DWG.

00:00:34.878 --> 00:00:36.852
A preview is rendered on a fixed page.

00:00:37.478 --> 00:00:40.494
Then the preview is laid over the source, to measure how far

00:00:40.494 --> 00:00:41.500
apart the lines are.

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:45.726
Here is the one worked example in the repository.

00:00:45.726 --> 00:00:47.605
Five entities on a small part.

00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:50.310
Each value says where it came from.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.632
Read off the drawing, scaled from pixels, inferred, or unreadable.

00:00:55.492 --> 00:00:56.771
Take a small plan.

00:00:56.771 --> 00:00:59.648
The model redraws the walls and types every dimension.

00:01:00.068 --> 00:01:01.820
Two are printed on the sketch.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.863
Six thousand, and two thousand four hundred.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:04.739
Those are known.

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:08.794
The rest are measured in pixels and scaled by an anchor, the

00:01:08.794 --> 00:01:10.309
nine hundred pixel overall width.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:13.326
The door is a guess at a standard size.

00:01:13.326 --> 00:01:15.923
The window's label is smudged, so it stays unreadable.

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:19.325
Now misplace one end of the anchor by four pixels.

00:01:19.325 --> 00:01:22.605
The wall becomes three thousand five hundred and eighty-four, and the

00:01:22.605 --> 00:01:23.798
chain stops adding up.

00:01:24.218 --> 00:01:26.148
That ledger is the right idea.

00:01:26.148 --> 00:01:28.400
But the drawing script never reads it.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.974
In the DWG, every dimension looks the same.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:33.816
Here is the formula.

00:01:34.692 --> 00:01:37.504
The slip is how far off one endpoint is, in pixels.

00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:40.441
The span is how long the anchor is.

00:01:40.441 --> 00:01:44.491
A four pixel slip on nine hundred is about zero point four four percent.

00:01:44.911 --> 00:01:46.453
Every scaled length shares it.

00:01:46.453 --> 00:01:50.771
On a sixty pixel column, the same slip is six point two five percent.

00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:54.906
Here it is in the article's own widget.

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:58.465
Slip the anchor by four pixels, and the scaled values drift while

00:01:58.465 --> 00:01:59.948
the chain check turns red.

00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:04.070
Calibrate on the short column instead, and the same slip throws every

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.317
scaled length much further.

00:02:05.697 --> 00:02:07.699
Then look at the drawing AutoCAD gets.

00:02:07.699 --> 00:02:08.843
The colours are gone.

00:02:08.843 --> 00:02:11.417
Nothing in the file says which numbers were guessed.

00:02:12.908 --> 00:02:16.462
The check fits the redraw onto the sketch at four corners,

00:02:16.462 --> 00:02:17.754
then measures edge distances.

00:02:17.754 --> 00:02:20.016
An exact redraw sits right on top.

00:02:20.396 --> 00:02:22.851
Now make every length three percent too long.

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:26.228
After the corner fit, it scores exactly like the perfect one.

00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:29.307
A wall in the wrong place does show.

00:02:29.307 --> 00:02:31.669
The ninety-fifth percentile jumps to thirty-eight pixels.

00:02:32.049 --> 00:02:35.676
A missing door shows only one way round, from the sketch to the redraw.

00:02:36.892 --> 00:02:41.372
Its own warning says the metrics assess registered visual linework, not

00:02:41.372 --> 00:02:43.002
physical dimensions or units.

00:02:43.002 --> 00:02:46.260
And the status it writes is always pass.

00:02:47.892 --> 00:02:49.365
So, the post's seven claims.

00:02:50.892 --> 00:02:54.314
Two hold as written: the drawing data, and AutoCAD drawing it.

00:02:54.314 --> 00:02:55.559
Five hold in part.

00:02:55.559 --> 00:02:58.981
Recognition is the model's, and the ledger stays in the JSON.

00:03:00.525 --> 00:03:03.583
For drawings to BIM, keep the tag on the element, so

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:04.695
it survives the file.

00:03:05.115 --> 00:03:06.759
Diff every view against its sheet.

00:03:06.759 --> 00:03:09.225
It catches a wall on the wrong grid line.

00:03:09.645 --> 00:03:13.408
And check the scale against printed dimension chains, because the diff cannot.

00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:16.335
Read it as a specification.

00:03:16.335 --> 00:03:19.700
Tag every number by where it came from, and never let a

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.224
picture that looks right stand in for a dimension.

00:03:24.108 --> 00:03:27.937
The model types the drawing, every value is tagged, and the check

00:03:27.937 --> 00:03:29.213
measures but never decides.

00:03:29.213 --> 00:03:30.170
Thanks for watching!

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.569
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:33.135
I'm Burl.

00:03:33.135 --> 00:03:33.418
Bye!
